<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.T3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Ablation of the core networks of MMHNet by comparing among transformers, Causal Mamba-2 and Mamba-2. Evaluation is performed on UnAV100 and LongVale datasets.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Transformers Vs. Causal Mamba-2 Vs. Non-Causal Mamba-2.</span> We also provide a comparison with different types of core networks in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2 Analysis and Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Echoes Over Time: Unlocking Length Generalization in Video-to-Audio Generation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Transformers are done without positional embeddings attached to the tokens. Causal Mamba-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> runs through tokens sequentially. Then, Non-causal Mamba-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> is used for our case to process long sequences and multimodal tokens more efficiently compared to causal Mamba-2.</p>
</div>
